  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 345 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry today outside of localized isolated rain \nchances near the coast, slightly lower humidity. \n\nEarly morning weather analysis indicates WNW flow aloft with a\nsubtle shortwave tracking across the region. At the surface, a\nweak trough is pushing to the coast and will move offshore\nthrough sunrise. There is no instability in place, with dew pts\nfairly comfortable by August standards (mostly in the 60s), so\nnothing more than a few sprinkles or light rain showers are \nexpected through the morning hours. For today, overall the\nmodels continue to show the local area in a large scale\nsubsidence zone as the shortwave moves offshore and low level\nflow remains mostly from the NW. Will have a few isolated spots\nnear the coast with PoPs ~15% this aftn as some of the CAMs hint\nat a few showers potentially developing with some low level\nconvergence/sea-Bay breeze influences. However, most areas, even\nat the coast will be dry and the CWA is not in any Day 1 SVR or\nERO risk. High temperatures will be close to normal, generally \nin the mid/upper 80s to lower 90s (warmest across interior \nsouthern VA and NE NC). Dew pts will not be too bad, mainly \nbetween 65-70, with locally high values along the immediate \ncoast. Therefore, peak heat indices will mostly only be in the \nlow-mid 90s. Another fast moving shortwave dives E-SE in the\nflow aloft tonight, and could set off a few isolated showers,\nmainly across the far north and eastern shore. Lows tonight\nmostly in the lower 70s with a few pockets of upper 60s possible\nin the interior rural areas. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Isolated-scattered showers/storms return later \nThursday into Thursday night, with the highest chances expected\nFriday afternoon-Friday night with a cold front. \n\nNW flow aloft continues Thu-Fri, with daily chances for\nshowers/storms returning Thursday as the airmass gradually turns\nmore humid once again with dew pts in the 70s. Still not a lot \nof focus for storms Thursday, so PoPs will remain capped at \n20-40%. SVR Weather is not anticipated as shear remains rather \nweak so any storms should be disorganized. Highs Thursday trend\nback into the lower 90s for most, with heat indices mostly back\ninto the upper 90s to lower 100s (approaching 105 along the\nAlbemarle sound). WPC has a Marginal Day 2 ERO risk across much\nof the area as PWATs increase back to ~2.00\". On Friday, the \nfront to the north is forecast to finally drop south into the \narea as the flow aloft further amplifies in response to a strong\nridge aloft across the lower MS Valley and a sharper trough \nover New England and eastern Canada. Instability ahead of the \nfront may be limited on Friday, especially for NE portions of \nthe FA, so even with decent shear, the SVR threat is uncertain \nand at this time, SPC does not even have the region in a \nMarginal SVR risk. Locally heavy rainfall will be possible given\nhighs PWATs but storms should be moving so unless the front \nstalls, a widespread heavy rain event is currently not expected \n(but with localized flooding possible). Temperatures Friday \ncould see a large gradient, with 80s to north and low-mid 90s \nwith heat indices 100-105 possible in NE NC. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mainly dry and slightly less humid Saturday, a\nfew showers and storms possible Sunday.\n\nSaturday looks mostly dry and fairly pleasant with highs in the\n80s, though more clouds and an isolated showers/storm will be\npossible in NE NC. The upper ridge starts to build back into the\nregion Sat night/Sunday, and some of the models even depict\nshowers Sat night. The NBM keeps that dry for now, but will have\na few showers/storms in the forecast for Sunday, along with\nhighs back around 90.",
